# Clean Code

Write code as if the next reader is a human. Prefer clarity over cleverness. Do not add comments that explain messy code — rewrite the code instead.

## Functions and modules

- One function, one reason to change (SRP). Extract when a name would be clearer than a block.
- Keep functions small. Prefer early returns over nested `if`/`else`.
- Side effects belong at the edges (I/O, navigation, storage). Pure logic stays pure and testable.
- Depend on abstractions the caller owns, not on concrete frameworks buried inside domain logic.

## Names

- Names reveal intent: `elapsedFocusMinutes`, not `t` or `data2`.
- No encodings (`strName`, `IUserMgr`). No abbreviations unless they are domain-standard.
- Functions are verbs; booleans read as predicates (`isExpired`, `hasActiveSession`).

```ts
// BAD
function handle(s: Session) {
  if (s.st === 1) {
    if (s.t > 0) doStuff(s);
  }
}

// GOOD
function resumeIfTimedSession(session: Session) {
  if (!session.isTimed || session.remainingSeconds <= 0) return;
  resumeSession(session);
}
```

## Structure

- DRY only when duplication shares one reason to change. Do not invent a shared helper for two similar-looking but different rules.
- Boy Scout Rule: leave the touched file cleaner than you found it — but do not refactor unrelated code.
- Delete dead code. Do not comment it out.

## Tests

- Tests specify behavior, not implementation. Name them for the scenario and expected outcome.
- One assertion theme per test. If setup is painful, the design is wrong — fix the API, not the test with mocks on mocks.
